WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.778
AI application architecture should name the major sources of instruction, data, identity, and action.

2
00:00:06.778 --> 00:00:08.395
Users provide requests.

3
00:00:08.395 --> 00:00:12.400
System and developer instructions define trusted behavior.

4
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:14.172
Prompts assemble context.

5
00:00:14.172 --> 00:00:22.490
Retrieved documents, uploaded files, tool outputs, and external API responses provide data with different authority levels.

6
00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:24.493
The model produces responses.

7
00:00:24.493 --> 00:00:32.503
Storage systems, logs, memory features, identity providers, and downstream integrations preserve or act on the results.

8
00:00:32.503 --> 00:00:35.507
Each boundary deserves a deliberate control.

9
00:00:35.507 --> 00:00:43.979
A trust boundary is crossed when data, instructions, identity, or control moves between components with different trust levels.

10
00:00:43.979 --> 00:00:47.599
A user's request is not the same as a system instruction.

11
00:00:47.599 --> 00:00:51.681
A retrieved document is not the same as trusted configuration.

12
00:00:51.681 --> 00:00:57.073
A tool result is not automatically more reliable than the service that produced it.

13
00:00:57.073 --> 00:01:00.384
A model response is not a policy decision by itself.

14
00:01:00.384 --> 00:01:09.319
Clear boundaries help the application decide what may influence answers, what may influence permissions, and what may trigger actions.

15
00:01:09.319 --> 00:01:12.862
Every data source should have a clear authority level.

16
00:01:12.862 --> 00:01:20.795
Trusted application configuration can define allowed tools, allowed retrieval sources, user roles, and safety policy.

17
00:01:20.795 --> 00:01:30.885
Untrusted content can provide evidence or user intent, but it should not grant itself permissions, rewrite the system rules, or authorize new data access.

18
00:01:30.885 --> 00:01:43.439
This distinction matters because natural language can look authoritative even when it comes from a document, email, ticket, web page, upload, or tool output that should be treated only as data.

19
00:01:43.439 --> 00:01:47.675
The architecture should also show where decisions are enforced.

20
00:01:47.675 --> 00:02:00.923
If the application decides whether a user can retrieve a document, call a tool, export data, or approve an action, that decision should happen in Python code or a trusted service, not only in prompt wording.

21
00:02:00.923 --> 00:02:14.325
The model can assist with interpretation and drafting, but application controls should enforce identity, authorization, tenant boundaries, tool permissions, logging, and high-impact approval paths.
